From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 18:42:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Cut the cost of an attribute write

Two independent costs on the same path, both measured on a 248 KB
snapshot of a heavy dashboard page.

`has_class` and `each_class` re-split the `class` attribute and called
`Atom::from` on every token, on every call. Interning hashes the string
and takes the global atom set lock, and these are the hottest callbacks
in the engine: `has_class` runs once per class selector per candidate
element, and `each_class` backs rule bucketing, the style bloom filter
and revalidation. That put `has_class` at 8.3% of a warm restyle's self
time, with another 10% split across `Atom::from`, `phf_shared::hash` and
`dynamic_set::Set::insert`. `ElementData` already pre-parses `id` into
an atom for exactly this reason; classes now get the same treatment,
derived at the four places that can write the attribute; construction,
`set_attribute`, `clear_attribute`, and `add_attrs_if_missing` (which
routes through `set_attribute`). Warm restyle drops 33% and
querySelectorAll 27%; parse pays ~1% to intern once up front.

Separately, every `set_attribute` stamped `restyle_subtree()` on the
element and its parent, so writing one `data-*` attribute that matched
no selector recascaded the whole parent subtree; a single write to
`<body>` cost 7.8ms, ~92% of it inside stylo's restyle traversal. The
precise machinery was already wired: `set_attribute` snapshots the
element and stylo's traversal calls `invalidate_style_if_needed`, which
diffs the snapshot against the invalidation map and hints only the
elements a selector can reach. The blanket hints ran on top and drowned
it out. The upstream TODO on the parent hint asked for exactly this
narrowing.

Narrowing exposed a latent bug in the snapshot, which is why that half
is one change and not two. `changed_attrs` was built from the
PRE-mutation attribute list, so adding a brand-new attribute left its
name absent and the invalidation map was never consulted for it
(`[data-open] .child` did not restyle when `data-open` was first set,
and the blanket restyle had masked it. Snapshotting now takes the name
being written and updates an existing snapshot's names rather than
discarding the second write in a frame. Recorded values stay as first
captured; those are the pre-mutation state the diff wants.
`clear_attribute` keeps its subtree hint) removal is left conservative.

Warm restyle after one attribute write: 7.8ms -> 0.34ms.

primary_styles also took a borrow for its presence check, dropped it,
and took a second to build the returned ref. Paint calls it per element
and again per glyph run, so it now borrows once.
